<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Co-operative Republic of Guyana</h1><p class="st-meta">1966 CE – present · capital Georgetown</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The only English-speaking country in South America, independent since 1966 and transformed in the 2020s by offshore oil.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-guiana">British Guiana</a> became independent as Guyana on 26 May 1966, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbes-burnham">Forbes Burnham</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-national-congress">People's National Congress</a> as prime minister. On 23 February 1970, the anniversary of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/berbice">Berbice</a> uprising, it became the Co-operative Republic of Guyana.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbes-burnham">Burnham</a> pursued co-operative socialism, nationalising the bauxite and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> industries, and ruled through manipulated elections until his death in 1985. Politics remained divided along ethnic lines between the largely Afro-Guyanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-national-congress">PNC</a> and the Indo-Guyanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-progressive-party-guyana">People's Progressive Party</a>. In 1978 the mass deaths at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonestown">Jonestown</a> commune of an American cult drew world attention. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheddi-jagan">Cheddi Jagan</a> won free elections in 1992.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exxonmobil">ExxonMobil</a> discovered large offshore oil fields in 2015, and production began in 2019, making Guyana one of the world's fastest-growing economies. Venezuela's claim to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/essequibo">Essequibo</a> region, two thirds of Guyana's territory, remains a source of tension.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/co-operative-republic-of-guyana.jpg" alt="Co-operative Republic of Guyana" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Whitney Smith · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Guyana.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1966 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Georgetown</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Forbes Burnham, Cheddi Jagan, Irfaan Ali</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Hinduism, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Guyanese Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guyana">Guyana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guyana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1996&amp;focus=co-operative-republic-of-guyana" aria-label="Co-operative Republic of Guyana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3070 1923 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Co-operative Republic of Guyana lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3390 2179L3390 2179"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3390 2179h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3390 2179h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3390 2179h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Georgetown. <a href="/atlas?year=1996&amp;focus=co-operative-republic-of-guyana">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
